the sentencing hearing for Army sergeant  Bowe Bergdahl resumes today after  emotional testimony Bergdahl walked away  from his post in Afghanistan and was  captured by the Taliban after five years  he was released in a prisoner exchange  Bergdahl pleaded guilty to desertion and  misbehavior before the enemy he says  that he left his post to report  misconduct at his base mark Strassmann  is at Fort Bragg North Carolina with the  evidence presented against him mark good  morning good morning in Bergdahl's case  that charge of misbehavior before the  enemy has to do with the way he put US  soldiers at risk when they went to look  for him no US soldier was killed in that  search but the court did hear from a  former Navy SEAL who was wounded Bowe  Bergdahl arrived at the Fort Bragg  courthouse where he came face to face  with men who went on dangerous rescue  missions to find him  army colonel Clinton Baker who commanded  Bergdahl's battalion at the time  testified one unit was on patrol for 37  days straight the only time you slept  was when you couldn't stay conscious  anymore he testified retired Navy SEAL  James hatch walks with a limp and a  service dog now but in 2009 he was part  of Bergdahl's rescue mission near the  Pakistan border he was shot in the leg  during that mission on the stand he  described the moments following his  injury it hurt really bad I thought it  was dead he survived but his military  career was over I felt like you know  maybe if I'd have done things a little  bit differently not gotten hurt  you know the mission wouldn't have  failed you know in 2015 hatch talked  about his injury and Bergdahl he needs  to know you know how much was risk the  judge is considering a motion to drop  the case over President Trump's comments  on Bergdahl on the campaign trail then  candidate Trump called him a traitor who  should be shot you know the old days  boom firing squad not so the judge ruled  in February that he has not been  influenced by President Trump adding  those comments were made when the  president was still a civilian candidate  but defense lawyers argued Trump brought  his campaign comments to the forefront  again last week I'm not gonna comment on  him but I think people have heard my  comments in the past  armie evaluators concluded that Bergdahl  suffered from a schizotypal personality  disorder when he walked off his post in  Afghanistan his lawyers want the judge  to consider that as he decides whether  to put Bergdahl behind bars for as long  as the rest of his life Nora Marc thank  you
 

